ures on using r  so here we have a call to glm  glm.fit we tell it direction is the  response  and the predictors are going to we're  going to use the lag variables so that  yesterday's  price previous day's price and so on  up to lag five and we'll also use volume  and we tell it to use family equals  binomial and so that tells glm to fit a  logistic regression model instead of one  of the many other models that can be fit  with glm okay so we execute that fit  and  oops  i've skipped a line here so there we go  and  there it's done it's two lines there and  then we do a summary  and the summary  tells you some things about the fit  and in this case  it's giving you p-values on each of the  coefficients the estimated coefficients  they're standard errors the z-score and  the p-value  and it seems like none of the  coefficients are significant here  again not a big surprise for these kinds  of data  it doesn't necessarily mean it won't  make a  be able to make  any kind of reasonable predictions it  just means that possibly these variables  are very correlated actually the plot  doesn't suggest that  anyway none are significant  and it gives the null deviance which is  the deviance just for the mean so that's  the log likelihood if you just use the  mean model and then the deviance for the  model with all the predictors in that's  the residual deviance  and there was a very modest change in  deviance it looks like four units  on um  on  how many degrees of freedom we've got  six degrees of freedom difference in in  degrees of freedom there  okay  so we can make predictions  from the the fitted model  and so we assigned to glm.probs the  predict  of glm.fit and we tell it type equals  response so this will make predictions  on the training data that we use to fit  the model  and it gives you a  vector of fitted probabilities we can  look at the first five  and we see they they're very close to  fifty percent  which is again not too surprising we  don't expect to get 